// Fixture: string_escaped_trailing_backslash
// Rule under test: GDDL Spec v4, String Literal Escaping -- `\\` inside
// a string literal resolves to a single literal backslash character.
//
// This is deliberately the TRAILING-backslash case (value ends in `\`,
// source ends in `\\` immediately before the closing quote) rather than
// a mid-string backslash, because that's the one case the spec itself
// cites as the whole reason `\\` needs to exist: "a string that happens
// to end in one (e.g. a Windows-style path fragment) becomes ambiguous
// with the sequence that precedes the closing quote." A mid-string `\\`
// (more content follows before the real terminator) is a strictly
// easier case for the tokenizer and doesn't exercise the actual
// ambiguity this fixture is about.
//
// This exact case was found BROKEN during implementation of the escape
// feature: both quote-boundary-detection sites in parser.py (the
// preprocessor and split_top_level_equals) used a naive single-character
// lookback (`s[i-1] != "\\"`) to decide whether a `"` was escaped. That
// check cannot distinguish "one backslash before this quote" (escaped,
// correct) from "two backslashes before this quote" (a COMPLETE `\\`
// pair, NOT escaped) -- it misidentified the real closing quote here as
// still-escaped and kept scanning, producing "unterminated string
// literal" for a source line that should parse cleanly. Fixed by
// replacing the lookback with proper backslash-run PARITY counting
// (even count, including zero, means the quote is a real boundary; odd
// count means the last backslash is unpaired and actively escapes it)
// -- the standard resolution for this exact ambiguity, applied at both
// sites. This fixture golden-locks the corrected behavior.

define Item
	path = string 40

Item WindowsPath
	path = "C:\\Users\\"

// Expected:
//   Compiles cleanly, no error. Source content between the quotes is
//   `C:\\Users\\` (11 characters: C, :, \, \, U, s, e, r, s, \, \),
//   which unescapes to 9 bytes of actual content (each `\\` pair
//   collapses to one literal `\`), comfortably under `string 40`'s
//   39-byte capacity.
//   WindowsPath.path == the literal 9-character sequence
//   `C:\Users\` -- ending in a SINGLE trailing backslash, not two.
//   Character-by-character: ['C',':','\\','U','s','e','r','s','\\']
//   -- 9 elements.
